script:
export const meta = {
  name: 'design-review-b',
  description: 'Design review lane B: swarms, Claude Code dynamic workflows and a frontier research/OSS sweep; context sharing, blackboard, subagents in nodes and auditability',
  phases: [{ title: 'Research', detail: 'swarms + frontier sweep; context/subagents/audit' }],
}
const COMMON = args.common
phase('Research')
const [swarm, ctx] = await Promise.all([
  agent(`${COMMON}
TASK (lane 3, swarms, dynamic workflows, frontier research and OSS): Explain precisely, with sources, how (a) agent swarms work (Kimi's agent swarm / K2-series parallel sub-agent orchestration, OpenAI Swarm and Agents SDK handoffs, Anthropic's multi-agent research system, AutoGen/AG2, CrewAI, LangGraph multi-agent and its Send API, MetaGPT, CAMEL, GPTSwarm, and any 2025-2026 swarm systems you find), and (b) how dynamic workflows in Claude Code work (the Workflow tool used in this very session: a deterministic JS script orchestrating subagents with agent()/parallel()/pipeline(), schemas for structured output, concurrency caps, resume by run id from a journal; plus subagents, Agent SDK and 'ultracode'). Read research/landscape-20261006/ first (R01-R11 already surveyed much; do not repeat, extend). Then sweep frontier research and OSS relevant to Foundry with WebSearch/WebFetch (use 'standard' mode, 'extended' only when thin): automatic workflow/agent design (ADAS, AFlow, MaAS, EvoAgentX, ScoreFlow, FlowReasoner, MASS, Darwin-Goedel style), workflow memory and reuse (Agent Workflow Memory, ExpeL, Voyager skill libraries, AgentKB), typed/structured LLM programming (DSPy, LMQL, Outlines, BAML, Guidance, Instructor), durable execution (Temporal, Restate, Inngest, DBOS, Hatchet, Prefect, Dagster), blackboard/shared-memory multi-agent work (LbMAS, blackboard MAS 2025), cascades/routing (FrugalGPT, RouteLLM, model cascades with verifiers), small-model judges/verifiers (Jev/TypeSafe, LLM-as-verifier, reward models), and anything 2026 that changes the picture. Verify claims from primary sources; mark unverifiable ones.
Write research/review-20261007/03-swarms-frontier.md: (1) how swarms and Claude Code dynamic workflows work, mechanically; (2) how they relate to Foundry (same/different/complementary) with a precise mapping table; (3) whether a 'swarm' should be in scope and in what form (an operator/pattern over existing IR vs a new subsystem), with the minimal additions needed; (4) for each relevant paper/OSS: what it does, evidence quality, what Foundry should adopt/test/ignore, licence; (5) the top 10 ideas ranked by expected capability gain per effort, each with a falsifiable experiment.`, { label: 'review:swarms-frontier', phase: 'Research', model: 'opus', effort: 'xhigh' }),
  agent(`${COMMON}
TASK (lane 7, context sharing, subagents in nodes, auditability): The owner asks how information flows: a shared message board with a shared understanding of state, some private notes, and how to manage subagent spawning inside nodes, with auditability and efficiency paramount. Read docs/THESIS.md, docs/ROADMAP.md §2.2 (context system: private by default, shared by contract), §2.9-2.10 (security, audit), §5.6 (blackboard), research/core-20261006/DESIGN.md (context compiler, receipts, labels, quarantine capacity), src/context.mjs, src/runtime.mjs, src/journal.mjs, src/store.mjs, src/verdict.mjs, src/ports.mjs, src/executors/*.mjs, and the AMP/ARC relation (THESIS §8).
Write research/review-20261007/07-context-subagents-audit.md: (1) a concrete design for shared state: a typed blackboard (append-only facts with provenance, labels and schemas; who may read/write; compare-and-swap; subscriptions/triggers; conflict resolution; size limits and summarisation), private node notes (scratch that never leaks unless promoted by contract), run-level and cross-run state (and how it maps to AMP's canonical memory without duplicating it), and how the context compiler selects board items per node (receipts); (2) subagents inside nodes: when they are justified (open-ended exploration, tool loops, code edits) vs overcomplication; a bounded 'delegate/executor' node kind with its own budget, tool allowlist, sandbox, typed result, depth limit, and journaled transcript; spawning limits and deadlock/livelock avoidance; how a node's subagents appear in the audit trail; (3) auditability: what is recorded today vs missing (events, journals, receipts, decision records, verdicts, costs), a per-run 'flight recorder' view, replay of any node, tamper evidence; (4) efficiency: token budgets for shared context, caching (prompt caching prefix design for nodes), avoiding context bloat; (5) security worst cases (prompt injection through the board, label laundering, a subagent exfiltrating, confused deputy) and mitigations; (6) a staged plan with tests.`, { label: 'review:context-subagents', phase: 'Research', model: 'opus', effort: 'xhigh' }),
])
return { swarm, ctx }
